 maintain these  communities online if you look at reddit  it's ostensibly designed to be a  democracy in fact it's probably one of  the most democratic websites online that  the users submit stories and then they  vote on what shows up on the front page  it's great but if you actually look at  the civic structure of Reddit it's not  actually a democracy it's actually more  like a monarchy because you sure you  have the users voting on stories  submitting comments and voting on those  as well then above that you have  moderator users and they have the power  to remove content to remove users things  like that but then on the top you have  the reddit staff and they're basically  the kings of this of this community they  have total control they can change the  code they can change the data they can  do anything and in fact they sort of  highlighted this about a year ago when  Steve Huffman decided to he's the CEO of  Reddit he decided to jump into the  Donald and manually edit some of the  comments of users in the Donald he  figured it'd be funny you know if they  trolled the trolls right and perhaps if  you didn't really know how websites work  you might be shocked to realize that  somebody on the right staff could just  change people's comments but I think  probably most of us here are  technologists and word intuitively aware  well of course they can do that they run  the server right  so that's dovetails into this next  question what is it about the web that  makes it like this why did the reddit  staff act as the king and why do they  have total Authority I think it flows  directly from this thick server design  the architecture of applications on the  web fix server is actually a technical  term it means that we put all the  business logic into the server and  actually as a result all of the  authority so if you diagram it it looks  a little bit like this  you got the thick server at the put up  at the top and you have the database in  there you have all the code and you have  all the authority placed in the server  and then you have the web browsers the  clients and you actually call them a  thin client thick server thin client and  the thin client is just a view of this  data just a rendering of what's  occurring on the server and anytime you  want to do something as one of the  clients you actually have to ask the  server to do it for you so all the  authority lives in the server and if you  have any authority as a user you're  actually just borrowing it from the  server in a way well what is it about  the web that makes it like that why are  the servers King there's nothing in the  nature of computers that make it this  way if you look at computers in a state  of nature hanging out next to each other  look at a server you look at a normal  desktop computer or a laptop sure the  server's more powerful but that doesn't  necessarily mean that the server ought  to be King sir doesn't have to be in  charge but that's how it is so why is  that if it's not in the hardware the  answer has to do with this it has to do  with the specifications and the  standards that define the web HTTP HTML  all the web AP is they encode this set  of behaviors this is a very clear  example of how technology doesn't just  interact with civics but it actually  drives civics it defines what the civics  structure of a community is going to be  in a way you could say that these  specifications are like a shared  contract  between us or perhaps even kind of like  a Constitution because they define how  our online society of computers are  going to work with each other there's  nothing in the hardware that does this  it's in the specifications it's in this  shared agreement it's these rules that  bind us together and define how our  computers will relate to each other and  it really is kind of like a contract or  Constitution because it's just an  agreement between the devices to follow  these rules it isn't a constitution like  the kind but these people made but it is  the kind made by people like this the  hackers that set the web in motion and  then produce documents that look like  this but it did produce specs like this  and there is a parallel there because  all of our computers everybody here we  use the image tag that's how that  happens so when we ask what gives the  server the right to be king over our  application well the answer is that it's  in the architecture it's how the web is  designed now it's not just the authority  model that's encoded in these  architectures it's also the individual  capabilities of everybody in the network  what can my computer do on the web  those questions are answered in the  specifications and this gets us back to  that ad you ever write what rights does  my machine have what rights to the  server have and so I'm going to try to  get us a term here an architectural  right this is a right that is given to  my device and it is encoded in the  architecture of the web or really of any  network let's go through some examples  to kind of make it intuitive here we  have a local area network we're gonna go  with a pretty natural design for a local  area network everybody can connect to  everybody else so we have Alice Bob and  Karla their machines can talk freely to  each other and so you might say that  there's an architectural right in this  design there's a right for everybody to  connect to everybody else you don't have  to make the land this way you might  decide that alice is actually much more  important and you can't maybe trust Bob  and Karla in your architecture so you  can do it like this where only Alice is  allowed to create new connections  and Bob and Karla they just have to  listen you definitely could do it this  way but if you did you'd basically have  a two-tiered architecture two kinds of  citizen Alice who has the right to  connect and Bob and Karla who do not  they can only listen let's look at  another example of the Internet  Internet's kind of like our Lynn because  the Internet has the same basic  principle everybody should be able to  connect to everybody else should be able  to connect to everybody else so you  could say that there is a right to  connect globally encoded in the design  of the internet in the architecture of  the internet again you didn't have to do  it this way you could do it like China  has done it oh well excuse me I just  want to point out sometimes the internet  doesn't work quite so well because  there's not enough IP addresses and  their firewalls that's what that slides  about now like I was saying you don't  have to do it this way you could do it  like China does it right you could set  up a global government firewall or you  could set up something like a municipal  server somewhere that you have to talk  to and ask permission to make a  connection that is how you could do the  internet we chose not to we chose to  make it so that everybody can connect  everybody else and I think that means  something really important it means that  there is a value set encoded in that  standard and all the standards that's  defined the web a right to connect  globally it is not a neutral decision  okay so we have an idea about how  architectural rights work let's take a  look at the web you might say there's  two kinds of citizen on the web like we  were talking about before you have the  client and you have the server and the  client is your browser or your user  agent so let's step through some of the  rights that each of these kinds of  citizens on the web have well the client  of course has the right to browse and go  from one website to another and visit  content of course I can do that clients  have the right to filter as well you can  run an ad Walker  you might also call this a right to  mutate because you could run extensions  and change things once you receive them  this is up to some interpretation but  I'd say those are the two that's really  what a client is allowed to do on the  web so 