ve a more perhaps  even democratic design and how its  governed could we make it so that the  client actually has the right to publish  instead of always depending on a server  to do it for them we make it even so the  client has the right to having an  identity so that my personal identity is  no longer tied to whatever happens to  Twitter  when we even give clients the right to  deploy the code so they could decide how  their applications should work now I  mentioned at the beginning of this talk  that I work on peer-to-peer networks in  this context peer-to-peer networks  really fascinate me  because unlike client-server networks  they connect nodes in a way such that  they all have the same capabilities the  rights are completely evenly distributed  and appear to pure network in fact  that's sort of implicit in the name  we're all just peers I think that has an  equalizing force across the network it'd  be easy in a way to make this talk  largely about free software because in a  lot of ways that's deeply connected this  ability to control our right to modify  how the experience works but I actually  think it's a little bit bigger than that  I think it's about our ability to decide  how code works as a community that makes  it a question of civics not just free  software every time we ask Twitter or  Facebook to make a change to their  moderation policies to their feet  algorithms even to their interfaces it's  actually kind of a big deal requires  those companies to take a risk to  potentially lose users or change  something that's fundamental to their  success in the past and as a result they  may not be willing to make those kinds  of risks well when users have the option  to develop the software completely on  their own to pursue their own needs and  problems and ideas they don't have to be  afraid that it might hurt their bottom  line they don't have to think in terms  of maximizing their engagement numbers  they can just think about solving their  own problems as technologists I think  it's our job not to solve problems for  people but to give them the tools to  solve those problems so that they can  decide what is best for them especially  in these contexts of these global  societies like the web is I think  perhaps it's easy to think of the web as  being somewhat fixed and in disabled  places if we've already experienced the  full history of the web but the web has  only been around for about 30 years so  what are the next 30 years gonna be like  are we going to continue to use Facebook  and Twitter is it gonna be the same  governance model are we still going to  have servers as king or are we as users  going to be defining much more of our  experience in the future really think  about it 30 years from now is it going  to be like this I believe in civic  engagement and I believe it in all kinds  legal rights and regulations are very  powerful and I'm extremely excited about  what the gdpr will do for our industry  but the legal system is not our only  option and as people who shape  technology and shape the web we have  powerful tools at our disposal  technology is not neutral it encodes a  set of values and as a political system  it encodes a set of Rights and so it'll  be up to us to decide what our unlike  online civic structures will be like and  I hope you take this time to consider  which structures of power and which  rights you're enabling and the  architectures that you build if they go  very much  [Applause]
 

